<article class="env-definition" id="01TP">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01TP">Definition <span data-tag="01TP">29.22.1</span>.</a> Let $f : X \to S$ be a morphism of schemes. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
We say that $f$ is of <em>finite presentation at $x \in X$</em> if there exists an affine open neighbourhood $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(A) = U \subset X$ of $x$ and affine open $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(R) = V \subset S$ with $f(U) \subset V$ such that the induced ring map $R \to A$ is of finite presentation. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We say that $f$ is <em>locally of finite presentation</em> if it is of finite presentation at every point of $X$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We say that $f$ is of <em>finite presentation</em> if it is locally of finite presentation, quasi-compact and quasi-separated. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>